#### 2. Absence of keyboard-operable volume/mute controls
- **Location**: `BuggyVideoPlayer.jsx:23-27`
- **Impacted Group**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users, users with auditory sensitivity / hyperacusis.
- **Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), SC 1.4.2 Audio Control (Level A).
- **Confidence**: HIGH
- **Why this matters**: The custom controls container renders only a play/pause button. Because native browser controls (`controls` attribute) are omitted on `<video>`, keyboard-only users cannot adjust audio levels or mute the video. If the video contains audio that plays for more than 3 seconds, the user has no keyboard mechanism to control volume independently of the system master volume.
- **Fix**: Add keyboard-accessible volume control slider (`<input type="range" min="0" max="1" step="0.1" aria-label="Volume" />`) and a dedicated mute/unmute button (`aria-pressed={isMuted}`).

---

### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)

#### 1. Static `aria-label` overrides dynamic button label and violates Label in Name
- **Location**: `BuggyVideoPlayer.jsx:24`
- **Impacted Group**: Screen reader users, speech-input / voice-control users.
- **Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.3 Label in Name (Level A), SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A).
- **Confidence**: HIGH
- **Why this matters**: Line 24 specifies `<button onClick={togglePlay} aria-label="Play or pause">{isPlaying ? 'Pause' : 'Play'}</button>`. Because `aria-label` overrides the element's subtree text content in accessible name computation, the button is announced perpetually as `"Play or pause, button"` on assistive technologies, hiding whether the video is actively playing or paused. Furthermore, speech recognition users dictating "Click Play" or "Click Pause" will experience command failure because the accessible name (`"Play or pause"`) does not match the visible text (`"Play"` or `"Pause"`).
- **Fix**: Remove the static `aria-label` entirely and rely on the visible text node (`{isPlaying ? 'Pause' : 'Play'}`), or dynamically update the `aria-label` to match the visible text, or implement `aria-pressed={isPlaying}` with a constant accessible name.

#### 2. Playback state desynchronization from DOM media lifecycle
- **Location**: `BuggyVideoPlayer.jsx:8-15`
- **Impacted Group**: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility, all users.
- **Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A).
- **Confidence**: HIGH
- **Why this matters**: `isPlaying` is mutated optimistically in React state on click (`setIsPlaying(!isPlaying)`). It does not listen to native media pipeline events (`play`, `pause`, `ended`, `waiting`). If playback fails (e.g., autoplay blocked, codec failure), pauses due to buffering, or reaches the end of the video stream, the button remains labeled `"Pause"`, communicating an incorrect playback state to assistive technologies.
- **Fix**: Bind event handlers directly on the `<video>` element:
  ```jsx
  <video
    ref={videoRef}
    src={videoSrc}
    onPlay={() => setIsPlaying(true)}
    onPause={() => setIsPlaying(false)}
    onEnded={() => setIsPlaying(false)}
  />
  ```

---

### Minor Findings

#### 1. Button missing explicit `type="button"`
- **Location**: `BuggyVideoPlayer.jsx:24`
- **Impacted Group**: Keyboard users, form users.
- **Citation**: HTML Specification / WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.
- **Why this matters**: Native HTML buttons default to `type="submit"`. If `BuggyVideoPlayer` is embedded inside a `<form>`, activating play/pause via Enter/Space will trigger form submission.
- **Fix**: Add `type="button"` to all media control buttons.

#### 2. Non-descriptive `aria-label` on `<video>`
- **Location**: `BuggyVideoPlayer.jsx:20`
- **Impacted Group**: Screen reader users.
- **Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A).
- **Why this matters**: `aria-label="Video player"` provides redundant and generic information since the role is already identified as video/media by AT. It fails to convey what video content is actually playing.
- **Fix**: Pass a descriptive `title` or content label prop (e.g. `title={videoTitle}`), or remove `aria-label="Video player"` if controls and surrounding landmarks describe the media.

---

### Enhancements

1. **Controls Landmark/Group**: Wrap `.controls` in a container with `role="group"` and `aria-label="Video playback controls"`.
2. **Seek/Timeline Scrubber**: Provide an accessible `<input type="range" role="slider" aria-label="Seek slider" aria-valuemin="0" ... />` for scrubbing.
3. **Transcript Link**: Provide a link or collapsible disclosure region below the player with an HTML text transcript for dual auditory/visual accessibility (WCAG 1.2.3).

---

### Perspective Alarm Levels

| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |
|-------------|-------------|----------------|
| Auditory access | **HIGH** | `captions` prop ignored; no `<track>` element; no caption toggle UI (WCAG 1.2.2). |
| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | No keyboard controls for volume/mute or timeline scrub; missing `type="button"` (WCAG 2.1.1, 1.4.2). |
| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Accessible name static (`"Play or pause"`) masking state and violating Label in Name; desynchronized state (WCAG 2.5.3, 4.1.2). |
| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | Custom controls container lacks defined visual focus indicators or contrast definitions. |
| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Inconsistent playback state communication if stream buffers or ends. |
| Low vision | **LOW** | Standard button element used; relies on CSS for hit target and focus ring. |
| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No forced auto-play or parallax transitions detected. |

---

### Multi-Perspective Notes

- **Screen Reader User**: Hears `<video>` announced with generic label `"Video player"`. When focusing the control button, hears `"Play or pause, button"` regardless of whether the video is running or stopped. Receives no notification of whether captions are available.
- **Keyboard-Only User**: Can tab to the Play/Pause button, but cannot mute audio if it starts playing at high volume, nor seek forward/backward.
- **Deaf / Hard-of-Hearing User**: Completely shut out from video dialog and audio cues because captions are not mounted in the DOM.
- **Speech-Input User**: Sees "Play" on the screen, commands "Click Play", but the command fails because the accessible name is hardcoded to "Play or pause".

---

### What's Missing

1. **`<track kind="captions">`**: Completely omitted from the `<video>` element.
2. **Closed Caption Toggle Button**: No interactive control to enable/disable or select subtitle tracks.
3. **Audio / Mute Control**: No slider or toggle to adjust or mute sound.
4. **Scrubber / Time Rail**: No way to check current time, duration, or seek.
5. **Media Event Listeners**: Missing `onPlay`, `onPause`, `onEnded`, `onTimeUpdate`, `onVolumeChange`.

---

### Recommended Implementation

```jsx
import React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

const AccessibleVideoPlayer = ({ videoSrc, captionsSrc, title = "Video" }) => {
  const [isPlaying, setIsPlaying] = useState(false);
  const [isMuted, setIsMuted] = useState(false);
  const [captionsEnabled, setCaptionsEnabled] = useState(false);
  const videoRef = useRef(null);

  const togglePlay = () => {
    if (!videoRef.current) return;
    if (videoRef.current.paused || videoRef.current.ended) {
      videoRef.current.play();
    } else {
      videoRef.current.pause();
    }
  };

  const toggleMute = () => {
    if (!videoRef.current) return;
    videoRef.current.muted = !videoRef.current.muted;
    setIsMuted(videoRef.current.muted);
  };

  const toggleCaptions = () => {
    if (!videoRef.current || !videoRef.current.textTracks[0]) return;
    const track = videoRef.current.textTracks[0];
    const newMode = track.mode === 'showing' ? 'hidden' : 'showing';
    track.mode = newMode;
    setCaptionsEnabled(newMode === 'showing');
  };

  return (
    <div className="video-player">
      <video
        ref={videoRef}
        src={videoSrc}
        title={title}
        onPlay={() => setIsPlaying(true)}
        onPause={() => setIsPlaying(false)}
        onEnded={() => setIsPlaying(false)}
      >
        {captionsSrc && (
          <track
            kind="captions"
            src={captionsSrc}
            srcLang="en"
            label="English captions"
            default={false}
          />
        )}
      </video>

      <div className="controls" role="group" aria-label={`${title} playback controls`}>
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={togglePlay}
          aria-label={isPlaying ? `Pause ${title}` : `Play ${title}`}
        >
          {isPlaying ? 'Pause' : 'Play'}
        </button>

        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={toggleMute}
          aria-pressed={isMuted}
        >
          {isMuted ? 'Unmute' : 'Mute'}
        </button>

        {captionsSrc && (
          <button
            type="button"
            onClick={toggleCaptions}
            aria-pressed={captionsEnabled}
          >
            CC
          </button>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default AccessibleVideoPlayer;
```

---

### Verdict Justification

**Verdict: REJECT**
- **Blocker 1 (WCAG 1.2.2 - Level A)**: Captions are not mounted or rendered, preventing auditory content access.
- **Blocker 2 (WCAG 2.1.1, 1.4.2 - Level A)**: No keyboard volume/mute controls.
- **Blocker 3 (WCAG 2.5.3, 4.1.2 - Level A)**: Static `aria-label` breaks accessible name synchronization and speech-input usability.

To achieve an **ACCEPT** verdict, the component must mount `<track kind="captions">`, provide accessible toggles for captions and audio/mute, remove static conflicting `aria-label`s, and synchronize React state directly with native HTML5 media events.
